module.exports = {
  title: 'Forms & HTTP',
  slug: 'forms-http',
  order: 6,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Template-driven vs Reactive Forms',
      slug: 'template-vs-reactive-forms',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Almost every real Angular application is, at its core, a machine for turning user input into HTTP requests. Login screens, checkout flows, settings panels, multi-step wizards — they are all forms. Angular ships <strong>two completely different form APIs</strong> to build them, and choosing the wrong one is one of the most expensive early mistakes a team can make: template-driven forms that grow past a few fields become impossible to test and reason about, while reactive forms sprinkled with <code>ngModel</code> create two competing sources of truth that fight each other on every keystroke.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers lean on this topic because your answer reveals whether you actually understand where the <em>form model</em> lives and who owns it. Candidates who can only say "reactive is better" without explaining <em>why</em> — synchronous availability, testability, dynamic controls, the <code>valueChanges</code> stream — get exposed immediately. Getting this right also prevents a whole class of production bugs: forms that validate too early, values that lag a keystroke behind, and controls that silently never register.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Both approaches ultimately build the same thing — a tree of <code>AbstractControl</code> objects (<code>FormControl</code>, <code>FormGroup</code>, <code>FormArray</code>) that holds the current value and validity of the form. The difference is <strong>who builds that tree and where</strong>.</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Template-driven</strong> — you write plain HTML with <code>ngModel</code> and Angular's directives quietly construct the control tree <em>for</em> you, after the view renders. The template is the source of truth. You import <code>FormsModule</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reactive (model-driven)</strong> — you construct the control tree explicitly in the component class with <code>FormControl</code>/<code>FormGroup</code>/<code>FormBuilder</code>, then bind the template <em>to</em> that existing model with <code>[formGroup]</code> and <code>formControlName</code>. The class is the source of truth. You import <code>ReactiveFormsModule</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>The mental model that never fails you: in template-driven forms the HTML <em>creates</em> the model; in reactive forms the HTML <em>connects to</em> a model you already made. That single inversion explains every practical difference between them.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-template-vs-reactive.svg|Side-by-side comparison of template-driven and reactive forms|Template-driven forms build the model from directives in the HTML; reactive forms build it explicitly in the class.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>In a <strong>template-driven</strong> form, the <code>NgForm</code> directive auto-attaches to any <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> and becomes the top-level <code>FormGroup</code>. Each <code>ngModel</code> registers a child <code>FormControl</code> — but crucially, <strong>this registration happens asynchronously, after the component's view initialises</strong>. That is why reading <code>form.value</code> in the constructor or <code>ngOnInit</code> gives you an empty object, and why template-driven validation state isn't reliably available until at least one change-detection cycle has run. The model is assembled behind your back, one directive at a time.</p>`,
        `<p>In a <strong>reactive</strong> form the tree already exists as a plain object graph the instant the class is constructed. There is no directive-driven registration step and no timing surprise — <code>this.form.value</code> is correct synchronously, before the template ever renders. Because the model is a normal JavaScript object you own, you can unit-test it with zero DOM: instantiate the component (or just the <code>FormGroup</code>), call <code>setValue</code>, assert on <code>valid</code>. Template-driven forms, by contrast, can only be meaningfully tested by rendering them and driving the DOM, because the model doesn't exist without the template.</p>`,
        `<p>Every control on both sides exposes the same <strong>state machine</strong>: interaction flags (<code>pristine</code>/<code>dirty</code>, <code>touched</code>/<code>untouched</code>) and a validity <code>status</code> (<code>VALID</code>/<code>INVALID</code>/<code>PENDING</code>/<code>DISABLED</code>). The decisive extra that reactive gives you is first-class observables — <code>valueChanges</code> and <code>statusChanges</code> — that you can <code>pipe</code> with RxJS (<code>debounceTime</code>, <code>distinctUntilChanged</code>, <code>switchMap</code>) to drive autocompletes, dependent fields and autosave. Template-driven forms technically expose these too, but reaching them means chasing directive references, so nobody does. This is why anything with cross-field logic, conditional validators, or reactive side effects belongs in a reactive form.</p>`,
        `<p>Dynamic structure is the other fault line. Adding or removing controls at runtime — a repeatable "add another phone number" list — is a natural <code>FormArray.push()</code> in reactive forms, but in template-driven forms it becomes a fragile dance of <code>*ngFor</code> plus <code>ngModel</code> that Angular's docs themselves warn against.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>A minimal <strong>template-driven</strong> form. Note there is no form model in the class at all — <code>NgForm</code> hands you one via a template reference variable:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { Component } from '@angular/core';\nimport { FormsModule, NgForm } from '@angular/forms';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-login-td',\n  standalone: true,\n  imports: [FormsModule],\n  template: \`\n    &lt;form #f=\"ngForm\" (ngSubmit)=\"submit(f)\"&gt;\n      &lt;input name=\"email\" ngModel required email&gt;\n      &lt;input name=\"password\" ngModel required minlength=\"8\"&gt;\n      &lt;button [disabled]=\"f.invalid\"&gt;Log in&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\`,\n})\nexport class LoginTdComponent {\n  submit(f: NgForm) {\n    // f.value is only populated because the view has rendered\n    console.log(f.value); // { email: '...', password: '...' }\n  }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The equivalent <strong>reactive</strong> form. The model is explicit, typed, and available before the template renders. Modern Angular (v14+) infers strict types from the initial values:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { ReactiveFormsModule, FormBuilder, Validators } from '@angular/forms';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-login-reactive',\n  standalone: true,\n  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule],\n  template: \`\n    &lt;form [formGroup]=\"form\" (ngSubmit)=\"submit()\"&gt;\n      &lt;input formControlName=\"email\"&gt;\n      &lt;input formControlName=\"password\" type=\"password\"&gt;\n      &lt;button [disabled]=\"form.invalid\"&gt;Log in&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\`,\n})\nexport class LoginReactiveComponent {\n  private fb = inject(FormBuilder);\n\n  form = this.fb.nonNullable.group({\n    email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]],\n    password: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.minLength(8)]],\n  });\n\n  submit() {\n    if (this.form.invalid) return;\n    console.log(this.form.getRawValue()); // typed, synchronous\n  }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The reactive advantage that has no template-driven equivalent — reacting to a value stream:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { debounceTime, distinctUntilChanged } from 'rxjs';\nimport { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';\n\n// inside the component, after form is defined\nconstructor() {\n  this.form.controls.email.valueChanges\n    .pipe(debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged(), takeUntilDestroyed())\n    .subscribe(email =&gt; this.checkAvailability(email));\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading <code>form.value</code> too early in a template-driven form.</strong> Controls register after the view initialises, so constructor/<code>ngOnInit</code> reads return an empty object.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mixing <code>ngModel</code> with <code>formControlName</code> on the same field.</strong> This creates two owners of one input; Angular used to allow it, now warns, and it produces baffling double-update bugs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the <code>name</code> attribute on <code>ngModel</code> inside a form.</strong> Without <code>name</code>, <code>NgForm</code> cannot register the control and it silently vanishes from <code>form.value</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Importing the wrong module.</strong> <code>[formGroup]</code> needs <code>ReactiveFormsModule</code>; <code>[(ngModel)]</code> needs <code>FormsModule</code>. Importing only one and using the other throws "can't bind" errors.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Choosing template-driven for a large, dynamic, or heavily validated form</strong> because it "looked simpler" in the first two fields — then paying for it when it becomes untestable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming template-driven forms can't validate.</strong> They can, via attribute directives (<code>required</code>, <code>minlength</code>); the real limitation is testability and dynamic structure, not validation.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Default to reactive forms</strong> for anything beyond a trivial, static, throwaway form. The predictability and testability almost always win.</li>`,
        `<li>Reserve <strong>template-driven</strong> for genuinely simple cases: a single search box, a newsletter email field, a quick prototype.</li>`,
        `<li>Never mix the two paradigms on the same control. Pick one per form and stay consistent.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>FormBuilder</code> (especially <code>fb.nonNullable</code>) rather than hand-newing <code>FormControl</code>s — less boilerplate and stronger typing.</li>`,
        `<li>Drive dependent fields and async checks through <code>valueChanges</code>, not template <code>(change)</code> handlers, so the logic lives with the model and is testable.</li>`,
        `<li>Bind button disabled state and error messages to control state (<code>form.invalid</code>, <code>control.touched &amp;&amp; control.errors</code>) rather than component booleans you maintain by hand.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the fundamental difference between template-driven and reactive forms?"</strong><br>A: Where the form model is built and owned. Template-driven forms build the control tree implicitly from directives (<code>ngModel</code>) in the HTML, asynchronously after the view renders — the template is the source of truth. Reactive forms build the tree explicitly in the class with <code>FormControl</code>/<code>FormGroup</code>, synchronously and before render — the class is the source of truth. Everything else (testability, dynamic controls, the <code>valueChanges</code> stream) follows from that.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why are reactive forms considered more testable?"</strong><br>A: The model is a plain object you construct in the class, so you can instantiate it, call <code>setValue</code>, and assert on <code>valid</code>/<code>errors</code> with no DOM at all. A template-driven model doesn't exist until the template renders, so testing it means rendering the component and driving the actual inputs.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "I read <code>this.myForm.value</code> in ngOnInit of a template-driven form and got {}. Why?"</strong><br>A: Because <code>ngModel</code> registers its controls <em>after</em> the view initialises, not during construction. In a template-driven form the model isn't ready in <code>ngOnInit</code>. A reactive form wouldn't have this problem — its value is available synchronously the moment the <code>FormGroup</code> is created.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Can you use <code>ngModel</code> and <code>formControlName</code> together on one input?"</strong><br>A: You shouldn't. It gives the input two owners fighting over its value; Angular warns against it and it causes double-update bugs. Choose one paradigm per form.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Which would you pick for a dynamic form where fields are added at runtime?"</strong><br>A: Reactive, without hesitation. <code>FormArray.push()</code>/<code>removeAt()</code> makes runtime structure a natural operation on a model you own. Doing the same in a template-driven form is a fragile <code>*ngFor</code>-plus-<code>ngModel</code> workaround the docs explicitly discourage.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Do both share the same validation and state model?"</strong><br>A: Yes — both build <code>AbstractControl</code>s with the same flags (<code>pristine</code>/<code>dirty</code>, <code>touched</code>, <code>VALID</code>/<code>INVALID</code>/<code>PENDING</code>) and the same <code>valueChanges</code>/<code>statusChanges</code> observables. Reactive just exposes them ergonomically; template-driven hides them behind directives.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Template-driven</strong>: <code>FormsModule</code>, <code>ngModel</code>, model built in HTML, async, less testable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reactive</strong>: <code>ReactiveFormsModule</code>, <code>FormControl</code>/<code>FormGroup</code>/<code>FormBuilder</code>, model built in class, synchronous, testable.</li>`,
        `<li>Template-driven: HTML <em>creates</em> the model. Reactive: HTML <em>connects to</em> the model.</li>`,
        `<li>Template-driven <code>value</code> is empty until the view renders; reactive <code>value</code> is available immediately.</li>`,
        `<li>Never mix <code>ngModel</code> + <code>formControlName</code> on one field.</li>`,
        `<li>Dynamic/large/cross-field logic ⇒ reactive. Tiny static field ⇒ template-driven is fine.</li>`,
        `<li>Both share the same <code>AbstractControl</code> state machine and observables.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Reactive Forms API — FormControl, FormGroup, FormArray & Validators',
      slug: 'reactive-forms-api',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>The reactive forms API is the practical toolkit you reach for on almost every serious Angular screen. Knowing that "reactive is better" is table stakes; being able to <em>compose</em> a control tree, wire cross-field validation, drive dependent fields from <code>valueChanges</code>, and update values without accidentally triggering an infinite loop is what separates someone who has shipped forms from someone who has only read about them.</p>`,
        `<p>This is also where subtle, expensive bugs live. The difference between <code>setValue</code> and <code>patchValue</code>, the meaning of <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code>, why <code>disable()</code> drops a field out of <code>value</code>, and why <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> can transform a janky form into a smooth one — these are exactly the details interviewers use to tell a mid-level engineer from a senior one. Master this API and you can build any form Angular can express, with validation that fires at the right moment and state that stays predictable.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Everything in reactive forms is an <strong><code>AbstractControl</code></strong> — the abstract base class with three concrete subclasses:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>FormControl</code></strong> — a single field. Holds one value, its validators, and its state flags.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>FormGroup</code></strong> — a fixed set of named child controls. Its value is an <em>object</em>: <code>{ email, password }</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>FormArray</code></strong> — an ordered, dynamic list of child controls. Its value is an <em>array</em>. Use it when the number of controls changes at runtime.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Groups and arrays nest arbitrarily, forming a tree. Validity and state <strong>bubble up</strong>: a <code>FormGroup</code> is <code>INVALID</code> if any descendant is invalid, and <code>dirty</code> if any descendant is dirty. You navigate the tree with <code>get('path.to.control')</code>. <code>FormBuilder</code> (via <code>inject(FormBuilder)</code>) is just a concise factory for building this tree — <code>fb.group()</code>, <code>fb.control()</code>, <code>fb.array()</code> — with <code>fb.nonNullable</code> giving you controls that reset to their initial value instead of <code>null</code>.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-formgroup-tree.svg|Tree of AbstractControls showing a FormGroup containing controls and a nested FormArray|A FormGroup nests FormControls and FormArrays; validity and dirty state bubble up from children to the root.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Each control runs a small <strong>state machine</strong> along two independent axes. The <em>interaction</em> axis tracks <code>pristine</code> vs <code>dirty</code> (has the value changed since creation?) and <code>untouched</code> vs <code>touched</code> (has the control been blurred?). The <em>validity</em> axis is the <code>status</code>: <code>VALID</code>, <code>INVALID</code>, <code>PENDING</code> (an async validator is in flight), or <code>DISABLED</code>. These axes are orthogonal — a field can be <code>dirty</code> and <code>VALID</code>, or <code>pristine</code> and <code>INVALID</code>. Error messages typically show only when a control is <em>both</em> invalid <em>and</em> touched (or the form is submitted), which is why you write <code>control.touched &amp;&amp; control.errors?.required</code>.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-form-state-machine.svg|The two axes of control state plus valueChanges and statusChanges streams|Interaction flags and validity status are independent axes; valueChanges and statusChanges emit after the model updates.}}`,
        `<p>Two observables expose the machine reactively. <strong><code>valueChanges</code></strong> emits the new value after each change; <strong><code>statusChanges</code></strong> emits the new <code>status</code>. Both fire <em>after</em> the internal model updates. Programmatic writes (<code>setValue</code>/<code>patchValue</code>) emit on these streams too — which is the source of the classic infinite loop: subscribing to <code>valueChanges</code> and calling <code>patchValue</code> inside the handler re-triggers itself. The escape hatch is <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code>, which updates the value silently.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Validators</strong> are just functions. A <em>synchronous</em> validator is <code>(control) =&gt; ValidationErrors | null</code> — it returns an errors object like <code>{ required: true }</code> or <code>null</code> if valid. An <em>async</em> validator returns a <code>Promise</code> or <code>Observable</code> of the same, and while it runs the control's status is <code>PENDING</code>. Critically, <strong>sync validators run first and gate the async ones</strong>: async validators only fire once all sync validators pass, which avoids hammering a server-side uniqueness check on obviously invalid input.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-validator-flow.svg|Flow showing sync validators running before async validators which set PENDING status|Sync validators run first; only if they all pass does the async validator run, moving the control from PENDING to VALID or INVALID.}}`,
        `<p>Two API distinctions catch people constantly. <strong><code>setValue</code> vs <code>patchValue</code></strong>: <code>setValue</code> is strict — you must supply <em>every</em> control's value or it throws; <code>patchValue</code> updates only the keys you pass and ignores the rest. <strong><code>disable()</code> vs a <code>disabled</code> attribute</strong>: calling <code>control.disable()</code> removes that control's value from <code>form.value</code> entirely (use <code>getRawValue()</code> to include disabled controls), and it also skips the control's validators. And <strong><code>updateOn</code></strong> — set per control or group to <code>'change'</code> (default), <code>'blur'</code>, or <code>'submit'</code> — decides <em>when</em> value/validation updates fire, a powerful lever for reducing validation noise.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>Building a nested tree with <code>FormBuilder</code>, including a <code>FormArray</code>:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { FormBuilder, Validators, FormArray, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';\n\n@Component({ selector: 'app-profile', standalone: true, imports: [ReactiveFormsModule], template: '...' })\nexport class ProfileComponent {\n  private fb = inject(FormBuilder);\n\n  form = this.fb.nonNullable.group({\n    name: ['', Validators.required],\n    email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]],\n    address: this.fb.nonNullable.group({\n      city: [''],\n      zip: ['', Validators.pattern(/^\\d{5}$/)],\n    }),\n    phones: this.fb.array([this.fb.control('', Validators.required)]),\n  });\n\n  get phones(): FormArray {\n    return this.form.get('phones') as FormArray;\n  }\n  addPhone() { this.phones.push(this.fb.control('', Validators.required)); }\n  removePhone(i: number) { this.phones.removeAt(i); }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A <strong>custom synchronous validator</strong> (field-level) and a <strong>cross-field group validator</strong> (must compare two controls, so it lives on the group):</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { AbstractControl, ValidationErrors, ValidatorFn } from '@angular/forms';\n\n// Field-level: no leading/trailing whitespace\nexport function noWhitespace(): ValidatorFn {\n  return (control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null =&gt;\n    control.value?.trim() === control.value ? null : { whitespace: true };\n}\n\n// Cross-field: password must equal confirm. Put this on the GROUP.\nexport const passwordsMatch: ValidatorFn = (group: AbstractControl) =&gt; {\n  const pw = group.get('password')?.value;\n  const confirm = group.get('confirm')?.value;\n  return pw === confirm ? null : { mismatch: true };\n};\n\n// wiring:\n// this.fb.group({ password: [''], confirm: [''] }, { validators: passwordsMatch })</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A <strong>custom async validator</strong> that checks username availability, debounced, returning an observable — note <code>PENDING</code> is automatic while it runs:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { AsyncValidatorFn } from '@angular/forms';\nimport { of, timer, switchMap, map, catchError } from 'rxjs';\n\nexport function usernameTaken(api: UserApi): AsyncValidatorFn {\n  return control =&gt;\n    timer(300).pipe( // debounce\n      switchMap(() =&gt; api.isTaken(control.value)),\n      map(taken =&gt; (taken ? { taken: true } : null)),\n      catchError(() =&gt; of(null)), // network error: don't block the user\n    );\n}\n\n// wiring: this.fb.control('', {\n//   validators: [Validators.required],\n//   asyncValidators: [usernameTaken(this.api)],\n//   updateOn: 'blur',   // don't fire on every keystroke\n// });</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Driving a <strong>dependent field</strong> from <code>valueChanges</code> without an infinite loop:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';\n\nconstructor() {\n  this.form.controls.country.valueChanges\n    .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed())\n    .subscribe(country =&gt; {\n      const state = this.form.controls.state;\n      country === 'US' ? state.enable() : state.disable();\n      // silent write avoids re-triggering this subscription\n      state.setValue('', { emitEvent: false });\n    });\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Infinite loop from <code>valueChanges</code> + <code>patchValue</code>.</strong> Writing inside a value subscription re-emits; use <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code> for the programmatic write.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>setValue</code> with a partial object.</strong> It throws unless every control is present. Use <code>patchValue</code> for partial updates.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting a disabled control in <code>form.value</code>.</strong> <code>disable()</code> excludes it (and skips its validators). Use <code>getRawValue()</code> when you need everything.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting a cross-field validator on a single control.</strong> A control can't see its siblings' values; comparison validators (password match, date range) must live on the parent group.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Async validator firing on every keystroke.</strong> Without <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> or an internal <code>timer</code>/<code>debounceTime</code>, you spam the server. Also always <code>catchError</code> so a failed check doesn't wedge the control in <code>PENDING</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading <code>errors</code> off the group when the error is on a child.</strong> <code>form.errors</code> only shows group-level errors; field errors live on <code>form.get('email').errors</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Rebuilding the whole <code>FormArray</code> to add one item.</strong> Just <code>push()</code>/<code>removeAt()</code>; recreating it loses state and subscriptions.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>FormBuilder.nonNullable</code> so controls reset to their initial value, not <code>null</code>, and types stay non-nullable.</li>`,
        `<li>Expose <code>FormArray</code>s through a typed getter (<code>get phones(): FormArray</code>) so the template stays clean and casts live in one place.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep validators as pure, reusable named functions in a shared file; compose them with <code>Validators.compose</code> or an array.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> (or <code>'submit'</code>) for expensive validation and noisy forms; keep <code>'change'</code> for instant feedback fields.</li>`,
        `<li>Always <code>catchError</code> in async validators and debounce them; a network hiccup must not trap the control in <code>PENDING</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Show errors only when <code>touched</code> (or after submit) to avoid yelling at users before they type.</li>`,
        `<li>Unsubscribe from <code>valueChanges</code>/<code>statusChanges</code> with <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code> or the <code>async</code> pipe to prevent leaks.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Difference between <code>setValue</code> and <code>patchValue</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>setValue</code> is strict — you must provide a value for every control in the group/array or it throws, which is great for catching shape mismatches. <code>patchValue</code> updates only the keys you pass and silently ignores missing ones, ideal for partial updates like pre-filling a couple of fields.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "My <code>form.value</code> is missing a field I can clearly see on screen. Why?"</strong><br>A: The control is almost certainly <code>disable()</code>d — disabled controls are excluded from <code>value</code> (and skip validation). Read <code>form.getRawValue()</code> to include them, or don't disable it (e.g. use <code>readonly</code> in the template instead).</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Subscribing to <code>valueChanges</code> and calling <code>patchValue</code> inside froze the browser. Fix?"</strong><br>A: You created an infinite loop — the programmatic write re-emits on <code>valueChanges</code>, which writes again. Pass <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code> to the write so it updates the model without firing the stream.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where does a 'passwords must match' validator go, and why?"</strong><br>A: On the parent <code>FormGroup</code>, not on either control, because a single <code>FormControl</code> can only see its own value. The group validator receives the group and can compare <code>group.get('password')</code> with <code>group.get('confirm')</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do sync and async validators interact?"</strong><br>A: Sync validators run first and synchronously. Async validators only run if all sync validators pass, and while they're in flight the control's status is <code>PENDING</code>. This ordering prevents firing an expensive server check on input that's already syntactically invalid.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> change?"</strong><br>A: It defers value and validity updates for that control (or group) until the field loses focus instead of on every keystroke. It's the standard fix for noisy validation and for throttling async validators without extra RxJS.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>FormControl</code> (one value) · <code>FormGroup</code> (object) · <code>FormArray</code> (dynamic list); all extend <code>AbstractControl</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>State: <code>pristine</code>/<code>dirty</code>, <code>touched</code>/<code>untouched</code>, status <code>VALID</code>/<code>INVALID</code>/<code>PENDING</code>/<code>DISABLED</code> — validity bubbles up.</li>`,
        `<li><code>valueChanges</code> / <code>statusChanges</code> emit after model updates; silence with <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>setValue</code> = all controls required; <code>patchValue</code> = partial.</li>`,
        `<li><code>disable()</code> drops the control from <code>value</code> and skips validators; use <code>getRawValue()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Sync validator: <code>(c) =&gt; ValidationErrors | null</code>. Async: returns <code>Observable</code>/<code>Promise</code>, sets <code>PENDING</code>, runs only after sync passes.</li>`,
        `<li>Cross-field validator ⇒ on the group. Debounce + <code>catchError</code> async ones. <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> to reduce noise.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'HttpClient — Requests, Observables & Error Handling',
      slug: 'httpclient',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p><code>HttpClient</code> is how an Angular app talks to the outside world. Every list you fetch, every form you submit, every token you refresh goes through it. But <code>HttpClient</code> isn't a thin <code>fetch</code> wrapper — it returns <strong>RxJS observables</strong>, and that single design choice is the source of both its power (composability, cancellation, retries, declarative error handling) and its most common bugs (requests that never fire, requests that fire twice, memory leaks).</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers love this topic because it sits exactly where two Angular pillars meet: HTTP and RxJS. A candidate who understands that an <code>HttpClient</code> observable is <strong>cold</strong> — that nothing happens until you subscribe, and that each subscribe is a <em>new</em> request — has internalised the model. One who calls <code>.subscribe()</code> everywhere, never handles errors, and wonders why their GET fires twice has not. Getting this right means fewer wasted requests, resilient error handling, and clean cancellation of stale calls.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>You inject <code>HttpClient</code> and call a verb method — <code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>. Each returns an <code>Observable</code> of the response body. Because it's an observable, <strong>the request does not start until something subscribes</strong>. Subscription happens either explicitly (<code>.subscribe()</code>) or implicitly (the template <code>async</code> pipe). The observable emits <em>exactly one</em> value — the parsed body — and then completes. Errors don't come through the value channel; they arrive as an <code>HttpErrorResponse</code> in the error channel, which is why you handle them with the observable's error callback or the <code>catchError</code> operator.</p>`,
        `<p>In modern standalone Angular (v17+) you enable it once at bootstrap with <code>provideHttpClient()</code> — no more <code>HttpClientModule</code>. That function is also where you register interceptors (<code>withInterceptors([...])</code>) and opt into features like <code>withFetch()</code>.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-httpclient-pipeline.svg|The HttpClient request pipeline from subscribe through interceptors to backend and back|An HttpClient call is a cold observable: subscribing sends the request through interceptors to the backend, and the response flows back, emits one value, and completes.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The defining property is that <code>HttpClient</code> observables are <strong>cold and unicast</strong>. Cold means the producer (the HTTP request) is created <em>per subscription</em>: calling <code>http.get(...)</code> builds a recipe, and each <code>subscribe()</code> executes that recipe from scratch. So subscribing twice fires two identical network requests — a real and common bug when a template <code>async</code>-pipes the same observable in two places. The fix is to share one execution with <code>shareReplay(1)</code>, or to subscribe once and store the value.</p>`,
        `<p>By default the observable emits the <strong>parsed body</strong>, typed by the generic you pass: <code>http.get&lt;User[]&gt;(url)</code> gives you an <code>Observable&lt;User[]&gt;</code>. If you need the status code or headers, pass <code>{ observe: 'response' }</code> to get the full <code>HttpResponse&lt;T&gt;</code>; for upload/download progress, <code>{ observe: 'events', reportProgress: true }</code> streams <code>HttpEvent</code>s. Query parameters go in <code>{ params }</code> (as <code>HttpParams</code> or a plain object) and headers in <code>{ headers }</code> — both are immutable, so builder-style methods return new instances.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Error handling</strong> runs through RxJS. A non-2xx response (or a network failure) pushes an <code>HttpErrorResponse</code> down the error channel. You intercept it with <code>catchError</code>, where you decide to (a) recover by returning a fallback observable (<code>of(defaultValue)</code>), or (b) rethrow with <code>throwError(() =&gt; ...)</code> so callers still see the failure. Distinguish the two failure classes: <code>error.status === 0</code> means a client/network problem (CORS, offline, DNS) and <code>error.error</code> is a <code>ProgressEvent</code>; a non-zero status means the server responded with an error and <code>error.error</code> is the server's error body.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Retries</strong> exploit the cold nature: <code>retry(2)</code> simply re-subscribes on error, firing the request again — perfect for idempotent GETs. The modern <code>retry({ count, delay })</code> form adds backoff, and you can gate retries to transient errors (e.g. only <code>status &gt;= 500</code>) with a custom <code>delay</code> function. <strong>Cancellation</strong> also falls out of the observable model: unsubscribing (or the <code>async</code> pipe tearing down, or <code>switchMap</code> switching to a new inner observable) aborts the in-flight XHR/fetch. That's why a <code>switchMap</code>-driven typeahead automatically cancels stale searches — no manual abort controller needed.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>Bootstrapping <code>HttpClient</code> (v17+) and a typed service:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// main.ts\nimport { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';\nimport { provideHttpClient, withFetch, withInterceptors } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { AppComponent } from './app.component';\nimport { authInterceptor } from './auth.interceptor';\n\nbootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {\n  providers: [\n    provideHttpClient(withFetch(), withInterceptors([authInterceptor])),\n  ],\n});</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { Injectable, inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { HttpClient, HttpParams } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { Observable } from 'rxjs';\n\nexport interface User { id: number; name: string; }\n\n@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })\nexport class UserService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient);\n  private base = '/api/users';\n\n  list(page: number): Observable&lt;User[]&gt; {\n    const params = new HttpParams().set('page', page).set('size', 20);\n    return this.http.get&lt;User[]&gt;(this.base, { params });\n  }\n\n  create(body: Partial&lt;User&gt;): Observable&lt;User&gt; {\n    return this.http.post&lt;User&gt;(this.base, body);\n  }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Robust error handling with typed branching, retry with backoff, and a fallback:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpErrorResponse } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { catchError, retry, throwError, timer } from 'rxjs';\n\ngetUser(id: number) {\n  return this.http.get&lt;User&gt;(\`/api/users/\${id}\`).pipe(\n    // retry transient failures with exponential backoff\n    retry({\n      count: 2,\n      delay: (err: HttpErrorResponse, i) =&gt;\n        err.status &gt;= 500 ? timer(2 ** i * 500) : throwError(() =&gt; err),\n    }),\n    catchError((err: HttpErrorResponse) =&gt; {\n      if (err.status === 0) {\n        // client/network error: err.error is a ProgressEvent\n        console.error('Network down', err.error);\n      } else {\n        // backend returned an unsuccessful code; err.error is the body\n        console.error(\`Backend \${err.status}\`, err.error);\n      }\n      return throwError(() =&gt; new Error('Could not load user'));\n    }),\n  );\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The <code>async</code> pipe (no manual subscribe, auto-unsubscribe) vs a stale-request-cancelling typeahead:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { shareReplay, debounceTime, distinctUntilChanged, switchMap } from 'rxjs';\n\n// Component: let the template subscribe. shareReplay stops a double request\n// if the async pipe is used in more than one place.\nusers$ = this.userService.list(1).pipe(shareReplay(1));\n\n// Typeahead: switchMap cancels the previous in-flight request automatically\nresults$ = this.search.valueChanges.pipe(\n  debounceTime(300),\n  distinctUntilChanged(),\n  switchMap(term =&gt; this.userService.search(term)), // stale calls aborted\n);\n// template: &lt;li *ngFor=\"let u of results$ | async\"&gt;{{ u.name }}&lt;/li&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Nothing happens because you never subscribed.</strong> A returned observable is a recipe; without <code>subscribe()</code> or an <code>async</code> pipe, no request fires. The #1 "my API call doesn't work" cause.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>The same request fires twice.</strong> Two <code>async</code> pipes (or two subscribers) on one cold observable = two requests. Share with <code>shareReplay(1)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Manual <code>subscribe()</code> without unsubscribing.</strong> Leaks — prefer the <code>async</code> pipe or <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code>. (HttpClient completes after one emit, so leaks are subtler but still real for progress/long streams.)</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No error handling at all.</strong> An unhandled <code>HttpErrorResponse</code> throws in the subscriber; always provide an error callback or <code>catchError</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Retrying non-idempotent requests blindly.</strong> <code>retry()</code> on a POST can create duplicate resources. Retry GETs and idempotent PUT/DELETE, not blanket POSTs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating <code>HttpHeaders</code>/<code>HttpParams</code> in place.</strong> They're immutable; <code>params.set(...)</code> returns a new object you must reassign.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing <code>status === 0</code> with a server error.</strong> Zero means the request never reached (or a response never came back from) the server — network/CORS — not an HTTP status the server chose.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Wrap HTTP in <code>@Injectable</code> services that return typed observables (<code>Observable&lt;User&gt;</code>); keep components free of raw URLs.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer the <code>async</code> pipe over manual <code>subscribe()</code> so unsubscription and change detection are handled for you.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>shareReplay(1)</code> for observables consumed in multiple template spots or shared across components to avoid duplicate requests.</li>`,
        `<li>Centralise cross-cutting concerns (auth headers, base URL, global error toast) in interceptors, not in every service.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle errors close to where you can recover; <code>catchError</code> to a fallback for UI resilience, or rethrow a typed error for callers.</li>`,
        `<li>Retry only idempotent requests, with backoff, and only on transient (5xx/timeout) failures.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>switchMap</code> for search/typeahead so stale requests cancel; use <code>concatMap</code>/<code>exhaustMap</code> when order or single-flight matters.</li>`,
        `<li>Enable <code>withFetch()</code> for the modern fetch backend (better SSR and streaming support).</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Are <code>HttpClient</code> observables hot or cold, and why does it matter?"</strong><br>A: Cold — the request is created per subscription and doesn't fire until someone subscribes. It matters because subscribing twice sends two requests (a real bug with multiple <code>async</code> pipes), and because unsubscribing cancels the in-flight request. To share one execution, use <code>shareReplay(1)</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "I called my service method but no network request happened. Why?"</strong><br>A: The method returns an observable and nothing subscribed to it. HttpClient observables are lazy; add <code>.subscribe()</code> or pipe it through <code>| async</code> in the template.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you distinguish a network failure from a server error in <code>catchError</code>?"</strong><br>A: Check <code>HttpErrorResponse.status</code>. <code>0</code> means a client-side/network failure (offline, CORS, DNS) and <code>error.error</code> is a <code>ProgressEvent</code>. A non-zero status means the server responded with an unsuccessful code and <code>error.error</code> holds the server's error body.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When is <code>retry()</code> dangerous?"</strong><br>A: On non-idempotent requests. Retrying a POST can create duplicate records. Also, blindly retrying 4xx errors is pointless — they won't succeed on retry. Gate retries to idempotent verbs and transient (5xx/timeout) errors, ideally with backoff.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Why does my typeahead sometimes show results for an old query?"</strong><br>A: You're probably using <code>mergeMap</code>, which lets all requests race, so a slow older one can resolve last. Use <code>switchMap</code>, which cancels the previous in-flight request whenever a new term arrives.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you read the HTTP status or headers, not just the body?"</strong><br>A: Pass <code>{ observe: 'response' }</code> so the observable emits the full <code>HttpResponse&lt;T&gt;</code> (with <code>.status</code>, <code>.headers</code>, <code>.body</code>). For progress events use <code>{ observe: 'events', reportProgress: true }</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>provideHttpClient(withFetch(), withInterceptors([...]))</code> at bootstrap — no <code>HttpClientModule</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Observables are <strong>cold/unicast</strong>: no subscribe ⇒ no request; two subscribes ⇒ two requests. Share with <code>shareReplay(1)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Emits one parsed body then completes; type via <code>http.get&lt;T&gt;()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>{ observe: 'response' }</code> for status/headers; <code>{ observe: 'events', reportProgress: true }</code> for progress.</li>`,
        `<li>Errors ⇒ <code>HttpErrorResponse</code> on the error channel; handle with <code>catchError</code>. <code>status === 0</code> = network/CORS.</li>`,
        `<li><code>retry({ count, delay })</code> only on idempotent + transient failures.</li>`,
        `<li>Unsubscribe cancels the request; <code>switchMap</code> auto-cancels stale calls.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer the <code>async</code> pipe; wrap calls in typed services.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'HTTP Interceptors — The Functional Middleware Chain',
      slug: 'http-interceptors',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Interceptors are Angular's answer to "how do I apply the same logic to <em>every</em> HTTP request without copy-pasting it into every service?" Attaching an auth token, prefixing a base URL, logging timings, showing a global loading spinner, retrying on 503, transforming responses, caching GETs — all of these are cross-cutting concerns that belong in one place, not scattered across fifty API methods. An interceptor is middleware for <code>HttpClient</code>: it sits between your service call and the network, and it can inspect or rewrite both the outgoing request and the incoming response.</p>`,
        `<p>This is a favourite interview topic because it tests three things at once: your grasp of the <strong>chain-of-responsibility</strong> pattern, RxJS operator fluency (the response is an observable you transform), and — post v17 — whether you're current with the <strong>functional</strong> interceptor API that replaced the old class-based DI approach. Getting interceptors right removes enormous duplication and makes auth, logging, and error handling consistent and testable. Getting them wrong produces requests that mutate each other, tokens attached to the wrong endpoints, or an accidental infinite loop of retries.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>An interceptor is a function that receives the outgoing request and a handler, does something, and forwards the request onward. In v17+ the shape is <code>HttpInterceptorFn</code>:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">export const myInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  // inspect or clone req here (outbound)\n  return next(req).pipe(/* transform the response here (inbound) */);\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>You register interceptors as an <em>ordered array</em> via <code>provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([a, b, c]))</code>. That order is the whole game: on the way <strong>out</strong>, the request passes through <code>a → b → c</code> then hits the backend; on the way <strong>back</strong>, the response bubbles through <code>c → b → a</code>. It's an onion — the first interceptor registered is the outermost layer, wrapping all the others. Each interceptor calls <code>next(req)</code> to pass control to the next layer; whatever you do <em>before</em> <code>next()</code> affects the request, whatever you pipe onto <code>next()</code>'s result affects the response.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-interceptor-chain.svg|Onion diagram of the interceptor chain with request flowing down and response flowing back up in reverse|Registration order is outbound order; the response flows back through the interceptors in reverse, like an onion.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The <code>HttpRequest</code> object is <strong>immutable</strong> — you cannot set headers on it directly. Instead you <code>req.clone({ ... })</code> to produce a modified copy and pass <em>that</em> to <code>next()</code>. This immutability is deliberate: it guarantees one interceptor can't corrupt the request another interceptor already saw, and it makes the chain predictable. Forgetting to clone (mutating and passing the original, or cloning but forwarding the original) is the single most common interceptor bug.</p>`,
        `<p><code>next</code> is an <code>HttpHandlerFn</code>: calling <code>next(req)</code> returns the <code>Observable&lt;HttpEvent&gt;</code> for the rest of the chain plus the backend. Because it's an observable, the <strong>response side is pure RxJS</strong>. You <code>pipe</code> operators onto <code>next(req)</code>: <code>tap</code> to log, <code>map</code> to reshape, <code>catchError</code> to handle failures, <code>retry</code> to re-attempt, <code>finalize</code> to hide a spinner whether the call succeeded or failed. The ordering rule falls straight out of this: code <em>before</em> <code>return next(req)</code> runs during the outbound pass; operators in the <code>.pipe()</code> run during the inbound pass, in reverse registration order.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Chain order matters functionally, not just cosmetically.</strong> A logging interceptor placed <em>before</em> the auth interceptor logs the request <em>without</em> the token; placed after, it logs it with the token. A caching interceptor must sit early enough to short-circuit (return a cached <code>HttpResponse</code> via <code>of(...)</code> instead of calling <code>next</code>) before the network layer. Because a functional interceptor is just a function that runs in an injection context, you can <code>inject()</code> services inside it — <code>inject(AuthService)</code>, <code>inject(Router)</code> — which is how the new API replaces the old constructor-injected class while staying tree-shakable.</p>`,
        `<p>A subtle point interviewers probe: interceptors run <strong>per request, per subscription</strong>. Because HttpClient observables are cold, if a request is retried (by <code>retry()</code> inside an interceptor or a service), the interceptor chain executes again for each attempt. That's why an auth interceptor can re-read a freshly refreshed token on a retry — and why careless retry logic can loop.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>A logging interceptor that times each request using <code>tap</code> and <code>finalize</code> (inbound side):</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpInterceptorFn } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { tap, finalize } from 'rxjs';\n\nexport const loggingInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  const started = Date.now();\n  console.log(\`→ \${req.method} \${req.urlWithParams}\`);\n  return next(req).pipe(\n    tap(event =&gt; { /* inspect HttpEvents/responses if needed */ }),\n    finalize(() =&gt; console.log(\`← \${req.method} \${req.url} (\${Date.now() - started}ms)\`)),\n  );\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A base-URL + header interceptor showing the mandatory <code>clone</code>, plus <code>inject()</code> inside a functional interceptor:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpInterceptorFn } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\n\nexport const apiInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  const config = inject(AppConfig); // injection context available\n\n  // Only rewrite our own relative API calls, not absolute third-party URLs\n  const isApi = req.url.startsWith('/api');\n  const apiReq = isApi\n    ? req.clone({\n        url: config.baseUrl + req.url,\n        setHeaders: { 'X-Client': 'web', 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      })\n    : req;\n\n  return next(apiReq); // forward the CLONE, never the original\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A caching interceptor that short-circuits GETs — note it does <em>not</em> call <code>next</code> on a cache hit:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpInterceptorFn, HttpResponse } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { of, tap } from 'rxjs';\n\nconst cache = new Map&lt;string, HttpResponse&lt;unknown&gt;&gt;();\n\nexport const cacheInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  if (req.method !== 'GET') return next(req);\n\n  const hit = cache.get(req.urlWithParams);\n  if (hit) return of(hit.clone()); // short-circuit: no network call\n\n  return next(req).pipe(\n    tap(event =&gt; {\n      if (event instanceof HttpResponse) cache.set(req.urlWithParams, event);\n    }),\n  );\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Registration — order defines the onion (logging outermost, cache innermost here):</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// main.ts\nprovideHttpClient(\n  withInterceptors([loggingInterceptor, apiInterceptor, cacheInterceptor]),\n)\n// Outbound: logging → api → cache → backend\n// Inbound:  backend → cache → api → logging</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting to clone.</strong> <code>HttpRequest</code> is immutable; <code>req.headers.set(...)</code> without <code>req.clone(...)</code> does nothing. Always clone and forward the clone.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Cloning but forwarding the original.</strong> <code>const r = req.clone({...}); return next(req);</code> — the clone is discarded. Pass <code>r</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Wrong order.</strong> Logging before auth logs tokenless requests; error handling placed inside a caching layer may never see network errors. Think through the onion.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Attaching auth to every URL.</strong> Adding your bearer token to third-party/absolute URLs leaks credentials. Guard with a URL check.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using the old class-based pattern in new code.</strong> The <code>HTTP_INTERCEPTORS</code> multi-provider + <code>HttpInterceptor</code> class still works via <code>withInterceptorsFromDi()</code>, but new v17+ code should use <code>HttpInterceptorFn</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Swallowing errors silently.</strong> A <code>catchError</code> that returns <code>of(null)</code> for everything hides real failures from callers. Rethrow unless you truly recover.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Injecting outside the injection context.</strong> <code>inject()</code> must be called synchronously at the top of the interceptor function, not inside a later async callback.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Keep each interceptor single-purpose (auth, logging, caching, errors) and compose them via the ordered array — easier to test and reason about.</li>`,
        `<li>Always <code>req.clone({ setHeaders / url / ... })</code> and forward the clone; treat the original request as read-only.</li>`,
        `<li>Deliberately order the array: auth before logging (log with token), caching early to short-circuit, global error handling typically outermost.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard token attachment and URL rewriting with explicit predicates so you never touch third-party endpoints.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>HttpInterceptorFn</code> (functional) for new code; it's tree-shakable and uses <code>inject()</code> instead of constructor DI.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>finalize</code> for spinner hide/cleanup so it runs on both success and error paths.</li>`,
        `<li>Unit-test interceptors with <code>HttpTestingController</code> and assert on the outgoing request's headers/URL.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the interceptor execution order for request and response?"</strong><br>A: Requests flow through interceptors in registration order (first registered runs first outbound); responses flow back in reverse order. It's an onion — the first interceptor is the outermost layer wrapping the rest. Whatever you do before <code>next(req)</code> is outbound; whatever you pipe onto its result is inbound.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "I set a header in my interceptor but the server never sees it. Why?"</strong><br>A: You almost certainly mutated the immutable request or forwarded the original instead of the clone. Headers/URL changes only take effect if you <code>req.clone({ setHeaders: {...} })</code> and pass the returned clone to <code>next()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How did interceptors change in v17+?"</strong><br>A: They moved from class-based (<code>HttpInterceptor</code> implementing <code>intercept()</code>, registered with the <code>HTTP_INTERCEPTORS</code> multi-provider) to functional <code>HttpInterceptorFn</code> functions registered via <code>withInterceptors([...])</code>. Functional interceptors are tree-shakable and use <code>inject()</code> for dependencies. The old style still works through <code>withInterceptorsFromDi()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you cache GET responses in an interceptor?"</strong><br>A: On a GET, check a keyed <code>Map</code>; on a hit, return <code>of(cachedResponse)</code> and skip <code>next()</code> entirely to short-circuit the network; on a miss, call <code>next(req)</code> and <code>tap</code> the <code>HttpResponse</code> into the cache. Place it early in the chain so it can short-circuit before the network layer.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can an interceptor stop a request or make a new one?"</strong><br>A: Yes. Not calling <code>next(req)</code> and returning your own observable (e.g. <code>of(cachedResponse)</code> or <code>throwError</code>) stops it. And inside <code>catchError</code> you can call <code>next()</code> again with a modified request — that's exactly how an auth interceptor retries with a refreshed token.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Why is my bearer token showing up in requests to a third-party API?"</strong><br>A: Your auth interceptor attaches the header unconditionally. Interceptors see <em>every</em> HttpClient request; guard with a check like <code>req.url.startsWith(this.apiBase)</code> so you only add credentials to your own backend.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>v17+: <code>HttpInterceptorFn</code> = <code>(req, next) =&gt; next(req).pipe(...)</code>; register with <code>withInterceptors([...])</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Chain is an <strong>onion</strong>: outbound in registration order, inbound in reverse.</li>`,
        `<li><code>HttpRequest</code> is immutable — <code>req.clone({ setHeaders, url, body })</code> and forward the clone.</li>`,
        `<li>Before <code>next()</code> = request side; <code>.pipe()</code> on <code>next()</code> = response side.</li>`,
        `<li><code>inject()</code> works at the top of a functional interceptor (injection context).</li>`,
        `<li>Skip <code>next()</code> + return <code>of(response)</code> to short-circuit (cache); <code>throwError</code> to abort.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard token/URL changes so you don't touch third-party endpoints; use <code>finalize</code> for cleanup.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Building an Auth Interceptor — Tokens, 401s & Refresh',
      slug: 'auth-interceptor',
      order: 4,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>The auth interceptor is the single most common real-world interceptor, and it's the one interviewers ask you to <em>build on a whiteboard</em>. Almost every authenticated Angular app needs to (1) attach a bearer token to outgoing requests and (2) recover gracefully when that token expires mid-session. Done well, users never notice their access token silently refreshed under a slow API call. Done badly, you get logged-out-mid-action bugs, a thundering herd of parallel refresh calls, infinite 401 loops, or tokens leaked to third-party domains.</p>`,
        `<p>This topic is the capstone of the chapter: it combines interceptor mechanics (clone, order, <code>next</code>), RxJS composition (<code>catchError</code>, <code>switchMap</code>, <code>throwError</code>, <code>shareReplay</code>), and real concurrency reasoning (what happens when five requests all 401 at the same instant?). A candidate who can implement token attachment plus a <strong>single-flight refresh</strong> that queues concurrent 401s demonstrates senior-level command of the whole HTTP + RxJS stack.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>An auth interceptor has two jobs. First, the easy one: for every request to <em>your</em> API, clone it and add <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;accessToken&gt;</code>. Second, the hard one: if the server replies <code>401 Unauthorized</code> because the access token expired, transparently use a longer-lived <strong>refresh token</strong> to get a new access token, then <strong>retry the original request</strong> with the new token — all without the user seeing anything. If the refresh itself fails, <em>then</em> you log the user out.</p>`,
        `<p>The trap that separates juniors from seniors is concurrency. When a token expires, it's rarely one request that 401s — it's every in-flight request at once (a dashboard firing five parallel GETs). A naive interceptor fires five refresh calls, five new tokens, and races. The correct design is <strong>single-flight</strong>: the first 401 triggers one refresh; every other 401 during that window <em>waits</em> for the same refresh to finish, then retries with the shared new token.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch7-auth-refresh-flow.svg|Flow of attaching a token then handling a 401 by refreshing once and retrying the original request|The interceptor attaches a token outbound; on a 401 it refreshes once, queues concurrent 401s behind that single refresh, and retries the original request with the new token.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Attaching the token</strong> is the outbound half. You read the current access token from an auth service and, guarding by URL so third-party calls are untouched, <code>req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${token}\` } })</code>. Because functional interceptors run in an injection context, you get the service with <code>inject(AuthService)</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Catching the 401</strong> is the inbound half, done with <code>catchError</code> on <code>next(authReq)</code>. You inspect the <code>HttpErrorResponse</code>: only <code>status === 401</code> triggers refresh; everything else (including 403, which means "authenticated but not allowed" — refreshing won't help) rethrows. You must also exclude the refresh endpoint itself from this logic, or a failing refresh produces its own 401 and you loop forever.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The refresh + retry</strong> uses <code>switchMap</code>: call <code>auth.refresh()</code>, and when it emits the new token, rebuild the original request with that token and call <code>next()</code> again. Because HttpClient observables are cold, re-invoking <code>next()</code> with the cloned request fires a genuine second attempt — the whole interceptor chain runs again for the retry.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Single-flight</strong> is the concurrency mechanism. You keep a shared, service-scoped observable of the in-flight refresh. The first 401 starts the refresh and stores it (typically a <code>BehaviorSubject</code> gate plus a <code>shareReplay(1)</code>'d refresh observable, or a null-token <code>BehaviorSubject</code> that requests wait on until it emits the fresh token). Concurrent 401s see a refresh is already running and <code>switchMap</code> onto the <em>same</em> refresh result instead of starting their own. When the refresh completes, the gate resets. If refresh fails, you clear tokens, redirect to login, and rethrow so no request silently hangs. This single-flight pattern — one refresh, many waiters — is exactly what interviewers are listening for.</p>`,
        `<p>One more subtlety: retry <strong>bounded, once</strong>. If the retried request <em>also</em> 401s (e.g. the new token is somehow rejected), you must not refresh-and-retry again. Track a flag or use a marker so a request is retried at most once, then give up and log out. Unbounded retry is the classic infinite-loop bug.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>The token-attaching half, guarded by URL:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpInterceptorFn } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\n\nexport const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  const auth = inject(AuthService);\n  const token = auth.accessToken;\n\n  // Never attach our token to third-party / auth endpoints\n  const isApi = req.url.startsWith('/api') &amp;&amp; !req.url.includes('/auth/refresh');\n  const authReq = isApi &amp;&amp; token\n    ? req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${token}\` } })\n    : req;\n\n  return next(authReq).pipe(/* 401 handling below */);\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The full interceptor with single-flight refresh and bounded retry. The <code>AuthService</code> holds the shared refresh state:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { HttpInterceptorFn, HttpErrorResponse } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { catchError, switchMap, throwError } from 'rxjs';\n\nexport const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {\n  const auth = inject(AuthService);\n  const token = auth.accessToken;\n\n  const isApi = req.url.startsWith('/api') &amp;&amp; !req.url.includes('/auth/refresh');\n  const authReq = isApi &amp;&amp; token\n    ? req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${token}\` } })\n    : req;\n\n  return next(authReq).pipe(\n    catchError((err: HttpErrorResponse) =&gt; {\n      // Only handle expiry on our API; ignore 403 and non-auth errors\n      if (err.status !== 401 || !isApi) return throwError(() =&gt; err);\n\n      // refresh$ is single-flight inside AuthService (see below)\n      return auth.refresh$().pipe(\n        switchMap(newToken =&gt; {\n          const retried = req.clone({\n            setHeaders: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${newToken}\` },\n          });\n          return next(retried); // retry ONCE with the fresh token\n        }),\n        catchError(refreshErr =&gt; {\n          auth.logout(); // refresh failed: end the session\n          return throwError(() =&gt; refreshErr);\n        }),\n      );\n    }),\n  );\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The single-flight <code>refresh$()</code> in the service — the piece that stops the thundering herd. A <code>BehaviorSubject</code> gates concurrent callers onto one shared refresh:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">import { Injectable, inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';\nimport { BehaviorSubject, Observable, filter, take, map, tap, finalize } from 'rxjs';\n\n@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })\nexport class AuthService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient);\n  accessToken: string | null = null;\n  private refreshing = false;\n  private token$ = new BehaviorSubject&lt;string | null&gt;(null);\n\n  refresh$(): Observable&lt;string&gt; {\n    if (this.refreshing) {\n      // A refresh is already in flight: wait for its result, don't start another\n      return this.token$.pipe(filter((t): t is string =&gt; t !== null), take(1));\n    }\n    this.refreshing = true;\n    this.token$.next(null);\n    return this.http.post&lt;{ token: string }&gt;('/api/auth/refresh', {}).pipe(\n      tap(res =&gt; {\n        this.accessToken = res.token;\n        this.token$.next(res.token); // release the queued waiters\n      }),\n      map(res =&gt; res.token),\n      finalize(() =&gt; (this.refreshing = false)),\n    );\n  }\n\n  logout() { this.accessToken = null; /* clear storage, redirect to /login */ }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>No single-flight guard.</strong> Every concurrent 401 fires its own refresh — a thundering herd that races and can invalidate each other's tokens. Queue waiters behind one refresh.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Infinite loop on the refresh endpoint.</strong> If <code>/auth/refresh</code> itself 401s and isn't excluded, the interceptor refreshes forever. Exclude it explicitly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Unbounded retry.</strong> Retrying more than once when the retried request also 401s loops indefinitely. Retry at most once, then log out.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating 403 like 401.</strong> 403 means authenticated-but-forbidden; refreshing the token won't grant permission. Only 401 (expired/invalid credentials) should trigger refresh.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Leaking the token to third parties.</strong> Attaching <code>Authorization</code> to absolute/external URLs sends your bearer token off-domain. Guard by URL.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading a stale token on retry.</strong> Rebuild the retried request from the <em>new</em> token returned by refresh, not the captured expired one.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>finalize</code> to reset the refreshing flag.</strong> If refresh errors and you don't reset the gate, all future refreshes are blocked and the app wedges.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Implement <strong>single-flight refresh</strong>: first 401 refreshes, concurrent 401s wait on the same shared observable, then all retry with the new token.</li>`,
        `<li>Exclude the refresh (and login) endpoints from token attachment and 401 handling to prevent loops.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle only <code>401</code> for refresh; rethrow <code>403</code> and everything else.</li>`,
        `<li>Bound the retry to a single attempt; on repeated failure, <code>logout()</code> and redirect.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard <code>Authorization</code> attachment to your own API origin so tokens never leak cross-domain.</li>`,
        `<li>Store the refreshing state and shared token in the auth service (with <code>finalize</code> to reset), keeping the interceptor thin.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer HttpOnly cookies for refresh tokens where possible; if using <code>localStorage</code>, understand the XSS trade-off.</li>`,
        `<li>Register the auth interceptor in a sensible position — usually before logging (so logs show the token) but such that global error handling can still see final failures.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Walk me through attaching a token and refreshing on 401."</strong><br>A: Outbound, clone the request and add <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> (guarded to my own API). Inbound, <code>catchError</code> the response; if it's a 401 on my API, call a single-flight <code>refresh$()</code>, then <code>switchMap</code> to re-clone the original request with the new token and call <code>next()</code> again. If refresh fails, log out and rethrow. Retry at most once, and exclude the refresh endpoint from all of this.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (the key trap): "Five requests 401 at the same moment. What happens with a naive interceptor, and how do you fix it?"</strong><br>A: Naively, each fires its own refresh — five refresh calls racing, potentially invalidating each other. The fix is single-flight: a shared flag/subject so the first 401 starts one refresh and the other four subscribe to the <em>same</em> refresh result (via a <code>BehaviorSubject</code> they wait on, or a <code>shareReplay(1)</code>'d refresh observable), then all retry with the one new token.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Why might an auth interceptor loop infinitely?"</strong><br>A: Two classic causes: (1) the <code>/auth/refresh</code> call itself returns 401 and isn't excluded, so the interceptor tries to refresh the refresh forever; (2) unbounded retry — the retried request 401s and you refresh-and-retry again with no limit. Fix: exclude the refresh endpoint and cap retries at one.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "401 vs 403 — do you refresh on both?"</strong><br>A: No. 401 means the credentials are missing/expired/invalid — refreshing may fix it. 403 means you're authenticated but not authorized for that resource; a new token won't change your permissions, so refreshing is pointless. Only 401 triggers refresh.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does re-calling <code>next()</code> actually send a second request?"</strong><br>A: Because HttpClient observables are cold — each subscription re-executes the request. When you clone the original request with the new token and return <code>next(retried)</code> from inside <code>switchMap</code>, that's a fresh subscription, so the whole interceptor chain and the network call run again.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where should the refresh token live?"</strong><br>A: Ideally an HttpOnly, Secure cookie so JavaScript (and XSS) can't read it; the browser sends it automatically to the refresh endpoint. Keeping it in <code>localStorage</code> is common but exposes it to XSS. The short-lived access token in memory, refresh token in an HttpOnly cookie, is the safer split.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Outbound: <code>req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${token}\` } })</code>, guarded to your API.</li>`,
        `<li>Inbound: <code>catchError</code> → if <code>status === 401</code> and not the refresh URL, refresh; else rethrow.</li>`,
        `<li>Refresh + <code>switchMap</code> → re-clone original with new token → <code>next(retried)</code> (cold ⇒ real retry).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Single-flight</strong>: one refresh, concurrent 401s wait on a shared <code>BehaviorSubject</code>/<code>shareReplay(1)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Retry <strong>once</strong>; exclude <code>/auth/refresh</code>; refresh fails ⇒ <code>logout()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Refresh on <strong>401 only</strong>, never 403. Reset the refreshing flag in <code>finalize</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Don't leak the token to third-party URLs. Prefer HttpOnly cookie for the refresh token.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
};
